## An agent evaluation guide separates successful outcomes from permitted actions

Source: iTechGuides — https://www.itechguides.com/how-to-prove-your-ai-agent-works-a-practical-evals-and-safety-guide/
Published: 2026-10-18
Channel: news

An October 18 iTechGuides guide proposes evaluating an agent against a versioned task contract before interpreting its success rate. The contract specifies the starting state, required outcome, available tools, permission limits, prohibited side effects, and acceptable escalation. Its worked refund example distinguishes a completed payment from the authorization and audit steps that should precede it. The article recommends inspecting the resulting payment state independently rather than trusting an agent’s completion message.

The practical instrumentation requirement is to preserve tool arguments, authorization decisions, returned values, retries, latency, and relevant state changes alongside agent, policy, fixture, and evaluator versions. Outcome checks and permission checks remain separate: a forbidden action can invalidate an otherwise successful run, while a safe escalation can respect policy without completing the customer’s request. The guide also recommends segmenting failures by severity and workflow so averages do not conceal costly exceptions.

This is instructional synthesis with an illustrative scenario, not a measured deployment study or a new standard. Its operational value depends on implementing reliable fixtures, independent assertions, and appropriately protected traces; recording a trace alone does not prove that the final outcome was correct.

Why it matters: Pair each consequential tool action with an authorization record and an independent state check; report task completion and policy compliance as separate results.

